1557
00:58:42,502 --> 00:58:44,862
- I'll make an order, sir. - Sure.

1558
00:58:45,672 --> 00:58:48,032
I'll get one olive cheese campagne,

1559
00:58:49,072 --> 00:58:51,471
one corn cheese jalapeno campagne,

1560
00:58:51,472 --> 00:58:52,611
- That looks good. - It's nice and salty.

1561
00:58:52,612 --> 00:58:54,011
- Campagne? - and one blue cheese baguette.

1562
00:58:54,012 --> 00:58:55,412
- Campagne. - I'll get those three.

1563
00:58:56,082 --> 00:58:58,582
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1564
00:59:00,712 --> 00:59:02,982
(He returns to his seat to eat.)

1565
00:59:05,122 --> 00:59:07,452
(While waiting for his sandwich...)

1566
00:59:07,792 --> 00:59:09,261
I'll go there next.

1567
00:59:09,262 --> 00:59:11,061
(He meticulously checks for the next place on the tour.)

1568
00:59:11,062 --> 00:59:12,062
Right.

1569
00:59:13,732 --> 00:59:15,831
(The sandwich is finally out.)

1570
00:59:15,832 --> 00:59:18,201
Here's the jalapeno melting cheese sandwich.

1571
00:59:18,202 --> 00:59:19,332
Thank you.

1572
00:59:20,972 --> 00:59:22,642
I really wanted to try this.

1573
00:59:24,202 --> 00:59:26,742
(He finally encounters the jalapeno melting cheese sandwich.)

1574
00:59:30,812 --> 00:59:32,141
(The melt cheese drizzles down.)

1575
00:59:32,142 --> 00:59:34,352
- There's steam coming out. - I love cheese.

1576
00:59:34,582 --> 00:59:36,652
Right? I love cheese and butter.

1577
00:59:37,422 --> 00:59:38,622
- Gosh! - Gosh!

1578
00:59:39,152 --> 00:59:40,422
There's the jalapeno.

1579
00:59:41,422 --> 00:59:43,861
(He feels touched.)

1580
00:59:43,862 --> 00:59:44,862
(Astonished)

1581
00:59:45,262 --> 00:59:46,391
- Awesome! - Gosh, that's amazing.

1582
00:59:46,392 --> 00:59:47,992
That's insane! Gosh!

1583
00:59:48,232 --> 00:59:49,462
I love cheese.

1584
00:59:49,862 --> 00:59:50,902
Awesome!

1585
00:59:54,132 --> 00:59:55,842
That looks so good!

1586
00:59:56,842 --> 00:59:58,672
(Smiling)

1587
00:59:59,512 --> 01:00:01,212
He looks so happy.

1588
01:00:01,442 --> 01:00:02,781
It was incredible.

1589
01:00:02,782 --> 01:00:03,782
- That must be good. - That looks so good.

1590
01:00:03,783 --> 01:00:04,882
Gosh.

1591
01:00:04,982 --> 01:00:07,312
I highly recommend that one.

1592
01:00:07,682 --> 01:00:09,382
That must be eaten there.

1593
01:00:10,722 --> 01:00:12,852
(He blows on the warm cheese.)

1594
01:00:14,492 --> 01:00:17,622
(This is the bite he has been waiting for.)

1595
01:00:19,262 --> 01:00:20,332
(Stretching out)

1596
01:00:21,062 --> 01:00:22,091
(Stretching out)

1597
01:00:22,092 --> 01:00:23,202
It's stretching out.

1598
01:00:24,062 --> 01:00:25,202
Gosh.

1599
01:00:26,672 --> 01:00:29,642
(Gosh, would you take a look at this piece of bread?)

1600
01:00:30,002 --> 01:00:31,741
(Looking carefully)

1601
01:00:31,742 --> 01:00:34,041
- What is this? - I'm so hungry right now.

1602
01:00:34,042 --> 01:00:35,572
(You can't be this good.)

1603
01:00:37,042 --> 01:00:39,282
Why at this hour? It's driving me crazy!

1604
01:00:43,582 --> 01:00:45,852
(This is it.)

1605
01:00:46,092 --> 01:00:48,221
The cheesy taste must be controlled with the jalapeno.

1606
01:00:48,222 --> 01:00:49,361
- Right. - Exactly.

1607
01:00:49,362 --> 01:00:51,092
The ciabatta has a clean taste.

1608
01:00:51,462 --> 01:00:56,162
The yellow cheese is mixed together with the mozzarella.

1609
01:00:56,502 --> 01:00:57,832
Say that while looking at Kian84.

1610
01:00:58,402 --> 01:01:00,931
(I see.)

1611
01:01:00,932 --> 01:01:01,932
(Not interested)

1612
01:01:01,933 --> 01:01:03,941
- Gosh, Kian84! - He's not interested.

1613
01:01:03,942 --> 01:01:05,201
(A world that Kian84 cannot understand at all)

1614
01:01:05,202 --> 01:01:09,481
But you can also taste the apple compote in between.

1615
01:01:09,482 --> 01:01:10,912
That's when you feel a sweet taste.

1616
01:01:11,112 --> 01:01:12,711
- It's a perfect harmony then. - It's perfect.

1617
01:01:12,712 --> 01:01:14,951
- It's perfect. - It's a triple beat...

1618
01:01:14,952 --> 01:01:16,921
with its sweet, salty, and spicy tastes.

1619
01:01:16,922 --> 01:01:19,203
- I can't imagine what it's like. - That must be so good.

1620
01:01:20,052 --> 01:01:21,592
All that running was worth it.

1621
01:01:24,322 --> 01:01:25,522
(Taking a bite)

1622
01:01:26,632 --> 01:01:27,962
He has a big mouth as well.

1623
01:01:28,192 --> 01:01:29,832
- He really enjoys eating bread. - Right.

1624
01:01:29,862 --> 01:01:32,372
It looks even better because he eats slowly.

1625
01:01:32,772 --> 01:01:34,671
It looks like he's actually tasting the food.

1626
01:01:34,672 --> 01:01:36,241
He's not just shoving it down his throat.

1627
01:01:36,242 --> 01:01:37,371
It looks like he's actually tasting the food.

1628
01:01:37,372 --> 01:01:39,141
(Turning his head)

1629
01:01:39,142 --> 01:01:40,441
- Why are you looking at him? - Why are you looking at me?

1630
01:01:40,442 --> 01:01:42,241
- No! Don't get me wrong! - Hey!

1631
01:01:42,242 --> 01:01:44,481
Do I just shove things down my throat?

1632
01:01:44,482 --> 01:01:46,751
- You enjoy food the best. - So you do taste the food.

1633
01:01:46,752 --> 01:01:48,512
But it does seem like you shove in the food.

1634
01:01:48,752 --> 01:01:50,922
- That looks so good! - My gosh.

1635
01:01:51,352 --> 01:01:54,322
(He takes the last bite while enjoying the taste.)

1636
01:01:57,222 --> 01:01:58,362
This is what happiness is.

1637
01:01:59,392 --> 01:02:01,032
Right. Happiness is right by us.

1638
01:02:01,262 --> 01:02:02,331
Right.

1639
01:02:02,332 --> 01:02:04,501
Bye.

1640
01:02:04,502 --> 01:02:05,631
Thank you.

1641
01:02:05,632 --> 01:02:07,512
(He heads outside after being filled with joy.)

1642
01:02:09,802 --> 01:02:11,742
Why does the world seem brighter?

1643
01:02:13,412 --> 01:02:14,671
My gosh.

1644
01:02:14,672 --> 01:02:17,152
(The world looks beautiful for Bread June after eating bread.)

1645
01:02:17,742 --> 01:02:18,881
Let's see.

1646
01:02:18,882 --> 01:02:19,882
(The Bread Tour continues.)

1647
01:02:19,883 --> 01:02:21,912
This is the perfect course!

1648
01:02:22,782 --> 01:02:24,121
But when you feel full,

1649
01:02:24,122 --> 01:02:26,251
doesn't it bring down the desire of wanting to go somewhere?

1650
01:02:26,252 --> 01:02:28,421
- No. I don't feel full with bread. - Right.

1651
01:02:28,422 --> 01:02:30,021
- Each place feels different. - Right.

1652
01:02:30,022 --> 01:02:31,582
My gosh. He's going into another place.

1653
01:02:32,562 --> 01:02:34,192
The bread smells amazing.

1654
01:02:35,902 --> 01:02:37,061
(The second place is a bread master's place.)

1655
01:02:37,062 --> 01:02:38,162
It's a master's place.

1656
01:02:38,562 --> 01:02:41,832
- Hello. - Hello.

1657
01:02:42,502 --> 01:02:44,502
(Jaw-dropping)

1658
01:02:45,302 --> 01:02:47,341
(The place you can see when you walk out from the first place)

1659
01:02:47,342 --> 01:02:48,772
Right, a place like that...

1660
01:02:49,112 --> 01:02:50,658
- Do you know that place? - Yes. It's Hyoja.

1661
01:02:50,682 --> 01:02:51,912
It's famous.

1662
01:02:52,812 --> 01:02:54,082
I don't think you can go there.

1663
01:02:54,352 --> 01:02:55,992
- Why not? - Because it means "good son."

1664
01:02:56,222 --> 01:02:57,382
(The bad son can only cry.)

1665
01:02:57,652 --> 01:02:59,121
(Only good sons should go there.)

1666
01:02:59,122 --> 01:03:00,191
Hyun Moo can't go there.

1667
01:03:00,192 --> 01:03:02,521
I guess I can't go there to follow the trend.

1668
01:03:02,522 --> 01:03:03,962
You need to go after the trend dies.

1669
01:03:05,092 --> 01:03:06,861
(He enters the bakery where bad son Moomoo can't go.)

1670
01:03:06,862 --> 01:03:07,962
No way.

1671
01:03:09,232 --> 01:03:11,362
Gosh, the smell of the bread is...

1672
01:03:13,032 --> 01:03:15,601
You know how a bakery like that has a different smell of bread?

1673
01:03:15,602 --> 01:03:16,672
Yes, I do.

1674
01:03:16,972 --> 01:03:19,442
I do. It has the smell imbued with history.

1675
01:03:20,042 --> 01:03:22,011
Their breads are very Korean.

1676
01:03:22,012 --> 01:03:23,082
Like mammoth bread.

1677
01:03:23,512 --> 01:03:25,311
- It'll come out warm. - The corn bread?

1678
01:03:25,312 --> 01:03:26,512
Yes. Have a good day!

1679
01:03:28,582 --> 01:03:30,482
- That corn bread. - What is that?

1680
01:03:31,822 --> 01:03:33,722
(Gulping)

1681
01:03:33,922 --> 01:03:35,962
Can I just grab this?

1682
01:03:36,092 --> 01:03:37,862
- I'll put it in for you. - Thank you.

1683
01:03:39,062 --> 01:03:41,231
(Straight into the cart as soon as he sees it)

1684
01:03:41,232 --> 01:03:42,832
- Here you go. Sure. - Thank you.

1685
01:03:45,132 --> 01:03:47,272
Gosh, when you smell the freshly baked bread...

1686
01:03:47,802 --> 01:03:49,742
(Gulping)

1687
01:03:50,442 --> 01:03:54,972
(Naturally grabbing another bread)

1688
01:03:55,742 --> 01:03:57,042
(Halting)

1689
01:03:57,412 --> 01:04:00,312
What do I do? I can't get all of these.

1690
01:04:00,852 --> 01:04:03,222
(But he gently places it on the tray.)

1691
01:04:04,282 --> 01:04:05,921
I can't help myself. Goodness.

1692
01:04:05,922 --> 01:04:08,151
(His head says no, but his hands won't stop.)

1693
01:04:08,152 --> 01:04:09,591
- I'll take these. - That's it?

1694
01:04:09,592 --> 01:04:10,622
Yes.

1695
01:04:11,522 --> 01:04:14,291
- Thank you. - Keep it open on the way.

1696
01:04:14,292 --> 01:04:16,132
- Okay. - Close it when it cools down.

1697
01:04:16,262 --> 01:04:18,431
- All right. Thank you. - Thank you.

1698
01:04:18,432 --> 01:04:20,372
Thank you so much.

1699
01:04:20,472 --> 01:04:22,131
(He filled his bag more fully with bread at the second place.)

1700
01:04:22,132 --> 01:04:23,402
Thank you!

1701
01:04:23,702 --> 01:04:25,242
I get why he brought his bag.

1702
01:04:25,872 --> 01:04:26,911
He doesn't know...

1703
01:04:26,912 --> 01:04:27,941
- when he'll visit again. - That's right.

1704
01:04:27,942 --> 01:04:29,411
- Since he's there... - He has to stock up...

1705
01:04:29,412 --> 01:04:30,932
- when he makes the trip. - That's it.

1706
01:04:35,982 --> 01:04:37,681
- What is it? - Is he going to smell it?

1707
01:04:37,682 --> 01:04:39,121
- He's eating one. - No, he has to eat one.

1708
01:04:39,122 --> 01:04:40,802
- He's eating one. - Of course. He has to.

1709
01:04:44,562 --> 01:04:45,791
How can you hold back?

1710
01:04:45,792 --> 01:04:47,792
(Taking a bite)

1711
01:04:49,302 --> 01:04:50,632
You call it EWW.

1712
01:04:51,202 --> 01:04:53,108
Eating the freshly baked bread. Eating while walking.

1713
01:04:53,132 --> 01:04:54,202
Eating while walking.

1714
01:04:54,432 --> 01:04:56,642
You should taste the freshly baked bread...

1715
01:04:56,702 --> 01:04:58,772
before it's put into the freezer.

1716
01:05:00,612 --> 01:05:03,512
(Quickly taking a bite while it's still warm)

1717
01:05:03,712 --> 01:05:05,282
I can even eat it with my gums only.

1718
01:05:06,382 --> 01:05:07,622
That'd be really uncomfortable.

1719
01:05:08,312 --> 01:05:12,151
There's onion and pepper in it.

1720
01:05:12,152 --> 01:05:13,322
The kick that pepper gives...

1721
01:05:13,822 --> 01:05:16,462
The pepper gives a kick between the soft bread.

1722
01:05:16,922 --> 01:05:18,662
- I should visit there to try it. - Yes.

1723
01:05:18,792 --> 01:05:20,461
- I should go too. - I'll take you guys.

1724
01:05:20,462 --> 01:05:21,991
- We should... - I think we should really go.

1725
01:05:21,992 --> 01:05:23,161
- Let's really go one day. - Let's go.

1726
01:05:23,162 --> 01:05:24,508
- We should really go. - Seriously. The bread...

1727
01:05:24,532 --> 01:05:26,071
- I think two of them won't go. - I'll go there.

1728
01:05:26,072 --> 01:05:27,172
Let's go by ourselves.

1729
01:05:27,732 --> 01:05:29,302
- Excluding these two? - Kian84.

1730
01:05:29,742 --> 01:05:31,642
Kian84, let's just have gukbap.

1731
01:05:32,472 --> 01:05:33,612
Let's just have pork gukbap.

1732
01:05:35,142 --> 01:05:38,182
(Bum June is highly satisfied with the bread.)

1733
01:05:39,252 --> 01:05:40,351
You're having a croquette too?

1734
01:05:40,352 --> 01:05:42,111
- That looks really good. - I prefer this.

1735
01:05:42,112 --> 01:05:43,281
- Me too. I prefer it too. - I prefer this.

1736
01:05:43,282 --> 01:05:45,651
(Crunchy)

1737
01:05:45,652 --> 01:05:47,522
Gosh, I'm really starving.

1738
01:05:48,452 --> 01:05:50,492
Did you see the batter?

1739
01:05:50,822 --> 01:05:52,092
- The crumbs... - The crumbs...

1740
01:05:52,422 --> 01:05:54,092
It's like a hedgehog.

1741
01:05:54,362 --> 01:05:55,832
It leaves your mouth chafed.

1742
01:05:57,202 --> 01:05:58,531
- You're eating one again? - He's having another one.

1743
01:05:58,532 --> 01:06:00,212
(The fourth place he came to to eat again)

1744
01:06:00,402 --> 01:06:01,431
(It's the Hong Kong bread place.)

1745
01:06:01,432 --> 01:06:03,672
- What's Hong Kong bread? - It's a pineapple bun.

1746
01:06:04,102 --> 01:06:05,202
Pineapple bread?

1747
01:06:05,972 --> 01:06:07,971
You can choose...

1748
01:06:07,972 --> 01:06:10,642
different kinds of butter and put it in between.

1749
01:06:10,782 --> 01:06:13,512
Isigny Butter, Echire Butter, Gourmet Butter.

1750
01:06:13,882 --> 01:06:15,852
- Oh, even Echire Butter? - Or President Butter.

1751
01:06:16,252 --> 01:06:17,321
You can choose one.

1752
01:06:17,322 --> 01:06:19,722
- They have different flavors. - That's right.

1753
01:06:19,822 --> 01:06:21,291
So that day, I got Isigny Butter and Echire Butter...

1754
01:06:21,292 --> 01:06:22,761
and put it in between.

1755
01:06:22,762 --> 01:06:24,921
Bum June, I'm learning a lot from you.

1756
01:06:24,922 --> 01:06:26,732
- I know. Seriously. - Seriously...

1757
01:06:26,862 --> 01:06:27,961
I really wanted to try it when I was in Hong Kong,

1758
01:06:27,962 --> 01:06:29,092
but I couldn't.

1759
01:06:29,632 --> 01:06:30,761
You can get it in Seochon.

1760
01:06:30,762 --> 01:06:31,762
(Jealous)

1761
01:06:32,202 --> 01:06:34,932
(Taking a big bite)

1762
01:06:36,142 --> 01:06:41,012
(A bakery pilgrimage trail where he savors butter properly)

1763
01:06:43,012 --> 01:06:45,511
I guess the bakeries are within walking distance.

1764
01:06:45,512 --> 01:06:47,011
Yes, I planned a route like that.

1765
01:06:47,012 --> 01:06:48,482
Oh, on purpose?

1766
01:06:48,652 --> 01:06:51,222
I find people who do a bakery pilgrimage amazing...

1767
01:06:51,422 --> 01:06:53,462
because you need to have fish cake soup in between.

1768
01:06:54,122 --> 01:06:56,321
I can't have all that because it's too choking.

1769
01:06:56,322 --> 01:06:58,221
Like a bowl of bean sprout gukbap we had during Grand Rainbow Bazaar.

1770
01:06:58,222 --> 01:06:59,332
Exactly!

1771
01:06:59,792 --> 01:07:00,991
One with crab.

1772
01:07:00,992 --> 01:07:02,231
- A sip of fish cake soup! - That sounds good.

1773
01:07:02,232 --> 01:07:03,301
You need to have...

1774
01:07:03,302 --> 01:07:05,178
- a cup of that before moving on. - By the way,

1775
01:07:05,202 --> 01:07:06,802
what was that? "Exactly!"

1776
01:07:07,032 --> 01:07:08,172
- I said that? - "Exactly!"

1777
01:07:09,102 --> 01:07:10,641
(Super excited at the thought of having fish cake soup)

1778
01:07:10,642 --> 01:07:12,042
I'd never heard that tone from you.

1779
01:07:12,872 --> 01:07:14,371
(Buzzing)

1780
01:07:14,372 --> 01:07:15,442
How pretty!

1781
01:07:17,112 --> 01:07:18,412
Hello.

1782
01:07:19,212 --> 01:07:20,412
Thank you.

1783
01:07:20,952 --> 01:07:22,081
(The ladies notice Bum June.)

1784
01:07:22,082 --> 01:07:25,222
You were recently in that drama. What's it called again?

1785
01:07:25,452 --> 01:07:26,752
- "Would You Marry Me?" - Yes.

1786
01:07:27,692 --> 01:07:28,892
I really enjoyed it.

1787
01:07:29,122 --> 01:07:30,222
I hated your character.

1788
01:07:31,508 --> 01:07:33,918
- Please give me your autograph. - Gosh, of course.

1789
01:07:34,378 --> 01:07:36,318
You can't even walk around the streets anymore.

1790
01:07:36,588 --> 01:07:38,518
So many people recognize me thanks to the drama.

1791
01:07:39,418 --> 01:07:40,988
- Ms. Hye Sook. - I'm really grateful.

1792
01:07:42,058 --> 01:07:43,157
You were annoying.

1793
01:07:43,158 --> 01:07:44,587
(His acting was really annoying.)

1794
01:07:44,588 --> 01:07:46,428
- You were really annoying. - Right.

1795
01:07:46,728 --> 01:07:48,867
- Become a superstar. - Thank you.

1796
01:07:48,868 --> 01:07:50,928
I'll work hard and show an even better performance.

1797
01:07:51,068 --> 01:07:53,028
Of course. I'm sure you'll be in a lot of dramas.

1798
01:07:54,368 --> 01:07:56,168
Let's shake hands. I hope you'll make it.

1799
01:07:56,368 --> 01:07:58,308
Ma'am, this is...

1800
01:07:58,578 --> 01:08:01,008
I just bought these.

1801
01:08:01,308 --> 01:08:04,207
- The croquette is really good. - Oh, my. How sweet of him.

1802
01:08:04,208 --> 01:08:05,277
- So I... - No, it's okay.

1803
01:08:05,278 --> 01:08:06,848
- No, you can have it. - You did well.

1804
01:08:07,018 --> 01:08:08,047
We'll buy it ourselves.

1805
01:08:08,048 --> 01:08:09,717
She said, "No." And he's going, "Have it."

1806
01:08:09,718 --> 01:08:11,017
The fact that he gave bread when he loves it so much...

1807
01:08:11,018 --> 01:08:14,187
means he was grateful toward the ladies.

1808
01:08:14,188 --> 01:08:16,027
- It's like he gave his everything. - He gave everything.

1809
01:08:16,028 --> 01:08:17,257
I don't know how to act like that.

1810
01:08:17,258 --> 01:08:18,558
I should learn to do it.

1811
01:08:19,258 --> 01:08:20,327
You've been saying that...

1812
01:08:20,328 --> 01:08:21,328
- for ten years. - I only know how to accept.

1813
01:08:21,329 --> 01:08:23,528
I've been hearing you say that for ten years.

1814
01:08:24,098 --> 01:08:26,397
- You're so pretty. - Thank you.

1815
01:08:26,398 --> 01:08:28,637
- Thank you. - Happy New Year.

1816
01:08:28,638 --> 01:08:30,007
(Star Bum June's heartwarming fan meet-and-greet)

1817
01:08:30,008 --> 01:08:31,037
- Mothers love croquettes. - Thank you.

1818
01:08:31,038 --> 01:08:33,407
- Right. Mothers love croquettes. - Happy New Year.

1819
01:08:33,408 --> 01:08:34,508
For sure.

1820
01:08:35,038 --> 01:08:36,108
All right.

1821
01:08:36,848 --> 01:08:37,948
He's going to another one?

1822
01:08:39,118 --> 01:08:40,278
Oh, it's here.

1823
01:08:41,678 --> 01:08:43,387
There must be a lot of places you want to visit around the world...

1824
01:08:43,388 --> 01:08:44,748
- because of bread. - That's true.

1825
01:08:44,848 --> 01:08:45,987
- Right? - Yes.

1826
01:08:45,988 --> 01:08:48,288
- Because I love bread... - If you go to Europe...

1827
01:08:48,688 --> 01:08:50,058
Hello.

1828
01:08:51,998 --> 01:08:54,028
(He's happy when he's just looking at bread.)

1829
01:08:54,328 --> 01:08:56,798
Pistachio chocolate croissant...

1830
01:08:57,028 --> 01:08:58,348
- I'll take three of that. - Yes.

1831
01:08:58,468 --> 01:09:00,867
Two kouign-amanns, two pain suisse,

1832
01:09:00,868 --> 01:09:02,544
- and one Paris-brest. - I've tried a kouign-amann.

1833
01:09:02,568 --> 01:09:03,568
- It's really good, right? - And one apple pie.

1834
01:09:03,569 --> 01:09:06,438
- Isn't it a round one with sugar? - That's right.

1835
01:09:06,778 --> 01:09:09,777
Except for apple pie, it's my first time hearing them.

1836
01:09:09,778 --> 01:09:11,338
It's my first time hearing all of them.

1837
01:09:11,448 --> 01:09:12,518
Yes.

1838
01:09:12,648 --> 01:09:14,578
(Buzzing)

1839
01:09:15,688 --> 01:09:16,788
(Glancing)

1840
01:09:17,648 --> 01:09:18,808
You can take a photo with me.

1841
01:09:19,518 --> 01:09:21,787
- Oh, you went there. - It was right there.

1842
01:09:21,788 --> 01:09:24,788
We came for the bakery pilgrimage.

1843
01:09:24,958 --> 01:09:26,827
- Bakery pilgrimage? - No way.

1844
01:09:26,828 --> 01:09:28,157
- They must be pilgrims. - Yes.

1845
01:09:28,158 --> 01:09:29,828
Pilgrims meet each other.

1846
01:09:30,128 --> 01:09:31,728
We came here from Daegu.

1847
01:09:31,928 --> 01:09:33,167
From Daegu?

1848
01:09:33,168 --> 01:09:34,197
Where are you visiting today?

1849
01:09:34,198 --> 01:09:35,607
We went to M bakery,

1850
01:09:35,608 --> 01:09:36,937
- and we went to S bakery. - Oh, my.

1851
01:09:36,938 --> 01:09:38,007
- Then we came here. - Oh, my.

1852
01:09:38,008 --> 01:09:39,568
They went to the same places I went to.

1853
01:09:39,778 --> 01:09:42,047
We're going to the bakery that Chef Park Joon Woo runs.

1854
01:09:42,048 --> 01:09:43,107
The one that opens at noon?

1855
01:09:43,108 --> 01:09:44,777
(I know that place.)

1856
01:09:44,778 --> 01:09:45,817
Oh, my.

1857
01:09:45,818 --> 01:09:47,217
Just hearing that...

1858
01:09:47,218 --> 01:09:49,187
It's my first time seeing a world like that.

1859
01:09:49,188 --> 01:09:50,418
I really wanted to go.

1860
01:09:50,548 --> 01:09:51,947
So there's a community like that.

1861
01:09:51,948 --> 01:09:53,734
- I didn't know it existed. - The line was too long.

1862
01:09:53,758 --> 01:09:55,827
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1863
01:09:55,828 --> 01:09:56,988
This is so much fun.

1864
01:09:57,688 --> 01:09:58,798
Would you...

1865
01:09:59,828 --> 01:10:01,127
- You bought a lot. - What?

1866
01:10:01,128 --> 01:10:02,367
Try this.

1867
01:10:02,368 --> 01:10:03,397
- What? - Can we really?

1868
01:10:03,398 --> 01:10:04,468
- Yes. - What?

1869
01:10:04,728 --> 01:10:07,397
One has Isigny Butter, and the other one has Echire Butter.

1870
01:10:07,398 --> 01:10:08,568
They must be really happy.

1871
01:10:08,908 --> 01:10:10,237
The pilgrims have met each other.

1872
01:10:10,238 --> 01:10:12,307
- Try it. It's good. - It's my birthday today.

1873
01:10:12,308 --> 01:10:13,537
I feel like I was given a birthday gift.

1874
01:10:13,538 --> 01:10:14,877
- It's your birthday? - Yes, to celebrate...

1875
01:10:14,878 --> 01:10:16,308
Happy birthday!

1876
01:10:18,048 --> 01:10:19,077
You know that, right?

1877
01:10:19,078 --> 01:10:20,277
There aren't any bad people...

1878
01:10:20,278 --> 01:10:21,624
- among those who like bread. - Yes.

1879
01:10:21,648 --> 01:10:23,918
There aren't any bad people among those who like bread?

1880
01:10:24,418 --> 01:10:25,617
Did you buy a lot...

1881
01:10:25,618 --> 01:10:27,657
- so you can share with people? - That's right.

1882
01:10:27,658 --> 01:10:28,727
- So he had a reason. - Really?

1883
01:10:28,728 --> 01:10:29,888
- I wondered why. - Actually,

1884
01:10:30,058 --> 01:10:31,058
I was happy while eating it.

1885
01:10:31,059 --> 01:10:32,627
But I got even happier...

1886
01:10:32,628 --> 01:10:34,697
after meeting people on the way.

1887
01:10:34,698 --> 01:10:38,128
Seeing them even happier when I shared the bread,

1888
01:10:38,528 --> 01:10:40,397
- I was really happy. - Your happiness was doubled.

1889
01:10:40,398 --> 01:10:42,067
He's a happiness ambassador who spreads happiness through bread.

1890
01:10:42,068 --> 01:10:43,708
At this rate, you can tour together.

1891
01:10:43,838 --> 01:10:46,108
- That'd be really nice. - Seriously.

1892
01:10:46,608 --> 01:10:48,577
So when I saw you on TV,

1893
01:10:48,578 --> 01:10:50,278
I thought you'd be a good man.

1894
01:10:50,378 --> 01:10:51,378
(You're the best.)

1895
01:10:52,048 --> 01:10:53,617
Seeing this, the word "pilgrim..."

1896
01:10:53,618 --> 01:10:55,148
- actually fits them. - You're right.

1897
01:10:55,388 --> 01:10:57,217
- Thank you. - Not at all.

1898
01:10:57,218 --> 01:10:58,447
Have a fun trip.

1899
01:10:58,448 --> 01:11:00,317
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1900
01:11:00,318 --> 01:11:01,688
- Have a safe trip. - Good luck!

1901
01:11:02,128 --> 01:11:04,587
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1902
01:11:04,588 --> 01:11:05,627
(Thanks to bread, he feels full at heart too.)

1903
01:11:05,628 --> 01:11:06,757
It's really fascinating.

1904
01:11:06,758 --> 01:11:08,598
(Thanks to bread, he feels full at heart too.)

1905
01:11:10,128 --> 01:11:11,427
All of that is bread, right?

1906
01:11:11,428 --> 01:11:13,037
- He's buying bread again? - Hello.

1907
01:11:13,038 --> 01:11:14,298
(Smiling broadly)

1908
01:11:15,868 --> 01:11:17,538
My goodness. It's the sixth place.

1909
01:11:18,038 --> 01:11:19,108
It's the sixth place?

1910
01:11:20,508 --> 01:11:22,278
The fact that they sell only one menu...

1911
01:11:22,878 --> 01:11:24,207
- There's only one menu on the list. - Exactly.

1912
01:11:24,208 --> 01:11:26,177
- It means they have a big pride. - That's right.

1913
01:11:26,178 --> 01:11:27,777
They focus on only one menu.

1914
01:11:27,778 --> 01:11:30,348
I like salt bread too.

1915
01:11:30,548 --> 01:11:32,708
- You like salt bread? - Yes, salt bread is delicious.

1916
01:11:32,788 --> 01:11:34,587
- It's simple and light. - That's right.

1917
01:11:34,588 --> 01:11:35,727
- You like salty bread. - Yes.

1918
01:11:35,728 --> 01:11:37,287
If you like light salt bread,

1919
01:11:37,288 --> 01:11:39,648
you can go to M bakery or B bakery, the ones you mentioned.

1920
01:11:39,928 --> 01:11:41,398
They have baguette-style salt bread.

1921
01:11:41,698 --> 01:11:44,104
But this place has salt bread that tastes like it's fried in butter.

1922
01:11:44,128 --> 01:11:45,598
(Realizing)

1923
01:11:45,698 --> 01:11:46,997
You don't even have to apply lip balm.

1924
01:11:46,998 --> 01:11:48,268
The juice from butter...

1925
01:11:48,538 --> 01:11:49,737
It makes your lips glossy.

1926
01:11:49,738 --> 01:11:51,307
Just like after we had gimmari.

1927
01:11:51,308 --> 01:11:53,707
- Exactly. It's like that. - After eating it...

1928
01:11:53,708 --> 01:11:55,108
Gimmari sounds good.

1929
01:11:55,608 --> 01:11:57,647
You fill your stomach and keep your lips glossy too.

1930
01:11:57,648 --> 01:11:59,048
Thank you.

1931
01:11:59,448 --> 01:12:00,518
Thank you.

1932
01:12:01,218 --> 01:12:02,318
It's full now.

1933
01:12:02,648 --> 01:12:04,368
- Could you give me your autograph? - Sure.

1934
01:12:05,118 --> 01:12:06,317
He has young girl fans too.

1935
01:12:06,318 --> 01:12:07,864
(A young girl fan approached Bum June, the star.)

1936
01:12:07,888 --> 01:12:09,687
Could you write, "Congratulations on graduating?"

1937
01:12:09,688 --> 01:12:10,757
- Of course. You graduated? - Yes.

1938
01:12:10,758 --> 01:12:12,858
- What's your name? - I'm Lee Ji Yeon.

1939
01:12:13,198 --> 01:12:14,957
- I enjoyed your drama. - Really?

1940
01:12:14,958 --> 01:12:16,168
Thank you.

1941
01:12:16,268 --> 01:12:19,368
They wanted me to sign their yearbook.

1942
01:12:19,898 --> 01:12:21,198
(Staring)

1943
01:12:21,368 --> 01:12:23,208
Guys, do you like bread?

1944
01:12:23,668 --> 01:12:24,668
Yes.

1945
01:12:24,669 --> 01:12:26,437
- You can share these. - No, it's okay.

1946
01:12:26,438 --> 01:12:27,677
Share them.

1947
01:12:27,678 --> 01:12:28,877
Congratulations on graduating.

1948
01:12:28,878 --> 01:12:29,948
(Bowing)

1949
01:12:30,308 --> 01:12:32,377
Thank you. Goodbye.

1950
01:12:32,378 --> 01:12:33,754
(The bread angel shared his bread three times.)

1951
01:12:33,778 --> 01:12:35,148
I want to run into you too.

1952
01:12:36,118 --> 01:12:37,947
Let's run into each other one day.

1953
01:12:37,948 --> 01:12:39,217
Seriously. Let's run into each other.

1954
01:12:39,218 --> 01:12:41,158
- Let's go on the bakery pilgrimage. - Yes.

1955
01:12:42,688 --> 01:12:44,288
(Happy with both hands full of bread)

1956
01:12:47,998 --> 01:12:49,127
(Dropping the bag)

1957
01:12:49,128 --> 01:12:50,497
The car will smell like bread.

1958
01:12:50,498 --> 01:12:51,567
That's right.

1959
01:12:51,568 --> 01:12:53,608
(Getting in the car filled with the smell of bread)

1960
01:12:54,138 --> 01:12:56,308
(Gosh, how tiring.)

1961
01:12:57,408 --> 01:12:59,308
(Laughing)

1962
01:13:00,738 --> 01:13:03,647
(A seat full of bread that makes him smile when he sees it)

1963
01:13:03,648 --> 01:13:04,947
It must be really good.

1964
01:13:04,948 --> 01:13:06,148
It's so French.

1965
01:13:06,318 --> 01:13:09,088
Do you have it for a week if you buy that much?

1966
01:13:09,148 --> 01:13:12,487
The bread I got is already gone. I finished them.

1967
01:13:12,488 --> 01:13:13,617
Really?

1968
01:13:13,618 --> 01:13:15,928
Because I shared it as well.

1969
01:13:15,958 --> 01:13:17,257
- Oh, you shared it? - Because there's bread...

1970
01:13:17,258 --> 01:13:18,997
for a meal and a dessert,

1971
01:13:18,998 --> 01:13:21,927
you can finish them quickly.

1972
01:13:21,928 --> 01:13:25,427
There's bread for a meal and a dessert separately in the West.

1973
01:13:25,428 --> 01:13:27,167
- That's right. - So it's...

1974
01:13:27,168 --> 01:13:29,407
I've never had bread as a dessert.

1975
01:13:29,408 --> 01:13:30,837
- I know. - It's like rice.

1976
01:13:30,838 --> 01:13:32,237
- That's right. - I consider it a meal.

1977
01:13:32,238 --> 01:13:34,077
Then do you not have bread for a dessert after a meal?

1978
01:13:34,078 --> 01:13:36,007
- I'd eat something like ice cream. - That's right.

1979
01:13:36,008 --> 01:13:38,247
Bread is a bit heavy.

1980
01:13:38,248 --> 01:13:40,348
- It makes you full. - But cakes are good.

1981
01:13:40,378 --> 01:13:41,677
- You don't eat it? - Cakes aren't that heavy.

1982
01:13:41,678 --> 01:13:43,918
- That's right. - Yes. Cakes can be eaten easily.

1983
01:13:44,488 --> 01:13:45,788
Don't try to convince me.

1984
01:13:45,918 --> 01:13:48,418
I also... I'm sorry. I don't know what I'm saying.

1985
01:13:49,588 --> 01:13:50,627
Then...

1986
01:13:50,628 --> 01:13:53,998
(Since I got the bread...)

1987
01:13:57,228 --> 01:13:58,327
Is that a plate?

1988
01:13:58,328 --> 01:13:59,937
- No, a portable table. - That's really convenient.

1989
01:13:59,938 --> 01:14:01,297
- That's right. - It's a must in a van.

1990
01:14:01,298 --> 01:14:03,184
- That's right. In a van. - Yes, a portable table in a car.

1991
01:14:03,208 --> 01:14:04,838
I usually have gukbap on it.

1992
01:14:04,938 --> 01:14:06,298
- That's incredible too. - Gukbap?

1993
01:14:07,208 --> 01:14:08,838
(Taking it out)

1994
01:14:09,578 --> 01:14:11,908
(He took out a paper box instead of gukbap.)

1995
01:14:16,218 --> 01:14:18,418
(This must be good.)

1996
01:14:19,818 --> 01:14:22,327
(Putting in the bread he specially chose)

1997
01:14:22,328 --> 01:14:23,658
Oh, you're arranging them?

1998
01:14:25,228 --> 01:14:27,298
It's set.

1999
01:14:28,228 --> 01:14:30,298
(The bread from famous bakeries are gathered.)

2000
01:14:31,128 --> 01:14:33,168
(Scribbling)

2001
01:14:34,038 --> 01:14:36,467
(Writing down in detail how to enjoy it)

2002
01:14:36,468 --> 01:14:39,538
Gosh, you're giving them away as a gift?

2003
01:14:39,938 --> 01:14:42,238
- You're so sweet. - Well done.

2004
01:14:42,448 --> 01:14:44,508
- His handwriting is really nice. - I know.

2005
01:14:44,708 --> 01:14:46,624
- It's really pretty. - We said the same thing...

2006
01:14:46,648 --> 01:14:48,647
when he wrote something at his sister's bakery.

2007
01:14:48,648 --> 01:14:50,047
We said it was handwriting that made our hearts pound.

2008
01:14:50,048 --> 01:14:51,548
(That's right.)

2009
01:14:54,358 --> 01:14:56,587
(Putting the bread with cold cream cheese in a cold pack)

2010
01:14:56,588 --> 01:14:58,128
Look how sensible he is.

2011
01:14:58,458 --> 01:14:59,527
I brought them all.

2012
01:14:59,528 --> 01:15:00,998
- In case they melt? - Yes.

2013
01:15:01,958 --> 01:15:02,967
All set.

2014
01:15:02,968 --> 01:15:04,874
(A gift set of famous bakeries' bread is complete.)

2015
01:15:04,898 --> 01:15:07,338
(He goes somewhere after packing the bread.)

2016
01:15:09,968 --> 01:15:13,608
Bread.

2017
01:15:14,708 --> 01:15:17,278
(Driving while humming excitedly)

2018
01:15:18,908 --> 01:15:19,948
Gosh.

2019
01:15:21,678 --> 01:15:23,648
(The place he arrived with excitement)

2020
01:15:23,788 --> 01:15:25,347
(Konkuk University)

2021
01:15:25,348 --> 01:15:27,117
- Konkuk University? - You didn't go there...

2022
01:15:27,118 --> 01:15:28,688
to have Konkuk Milk, did you?

2023
01:15:28,958 --> 01:15:30,527
Konkuk Milk?

2024
01:15:30,528 --> 01:15:32,358
To have Konkuk Milk with bread.

2025
01:15:32,798 --> 01:15:35,428
(Laughing)

2026
01:15:36,268 --> 01:15:38,898
(Looking around to find the milk to have with bread)

2027
01:15:39,598 --> 01:15:41,938
(Taking something out)

2028
01:15:42,738 --> 01:15:44,768
(Scurrying while relying on the map)

2029
01:15:46,338 --> 01:15:49,278
He must be going there to meet someone at Konkuk University.

2030
01:15:54,518 --> 01:15:55,918
He looks a bit nervous.

2031
01:15:57,648 --> 01:15:58,787
(Nervous)

2032
01:15:58,788 --> 01:16:00,187
He must be meeting a professor, seeing his face.

2033
01:16:00,188 --> 01:16:02,017
- Right. - It must be a senior he acted with.

2034
01:16:02,018 --> 01:16:04,004
- And he became a professor. - Right. I'm 100 percent sure.

2035
01:16:04,028 --> 01:16:05,257
- You're 100 percent sure? - For sure.

2036
01:16:05,258 --> 01:16:06,298
All right. Let's go in.

2037
01:16:08,328 --> 01:16:10,227
(He carefully knocks on the door after hesitating.)

2038
01:16:10,228 --> 01:16:11,968
(Exhaling)

2039
01:16:12,998 --> 01:16:14,167
(Who did Bum June come to see?)

2040
01:16:14,168 --> 01:16:16,138
Hello, sir.

2041
01:16:16,973 --> 01:16:18,912
(Bum June came to see someone.)

2042
01:16:18,913 --> 01:16:20,743
(Nervous)

2043
01:16:21,843 --> 01:16:23,742
(He carefully knocks on the door after hesitating.)

2044
01:16:23,743 --> 01:16:25,413
(Exhaling)

2045
01:16:27,483 --> 01:16:29,082
Come on in.

2046
01:16:29,083 --> 01:16:31,022
- Hello, sir. - Hello.

2047
01:16:31,023 --> 01:16:32,983
(Bum June's forever role model, Actor Yoo Ji Tae)

2048
01:16:34,853 --> 01:16:37,823
(He was in Korea's iconic movie.)

2049
01:16:38,663 --> 01:16:40,863
(His acting skill is always good.)

2050
01:16:41,763 --> 01:16:44,563
(Actor with 28 years of experience who's guaranteed to deliver)

2051
01:16:45,833 --> 01:16:46,833
(He's famous for his skills as a director as well.)

2052
01:16:46,834 --> 01:16:48,943
- Oh, he directs movies too? - Yes.

2053
01:16:49,303 --> 01:16:52,173
(He's appointed as a full-time professor at Konkuk University.)

2054
01:16:52,513 --> 01:16:54,413
(And 10 years ago)

2055
01:16:55,313 --> 01:16:58,013
(Aspiring actor Bum June who was working part-time)

2056
01:16:58,713 --> 01:17:00,552
- That's right. Back then... - Oh, my!

2057
01:17:00,553 --> 01:17:02,253
I know that content.

2058
01:17:03,123 --> 01:17:05,492
(Hi, Bum June.)

2059
01:17:05,493 --> 01:17:06,922
- Oh, my. - I was so young, right?

2060
01:17:06,923 --> 01:17:08,052
Look at his eyes.

2061
01:17:08,053 --> 01:17:10,823
(The meeting that made his dream rock solid)

2062
01:17:11,593 --> 01:17:14,293
(Actors live and die by style.)

2063
01:17:15,633 --> 01:17:18,433
(Fully recharged good energy from the senior he admires)

